     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That is twice the maximum prescription dose for adults, which is 3,200 mg/day. At that level the risk of serious GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events goes up sharply, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThe 1600 mg figure may be a total daily dose, or it may be a mix-up with another dose. Common adult oral regimens are:\n- **400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours**, as needed or scheduled, for pain or inflammation. The maximum is 3,200 mg/day, and the lowest effective dose is preferred.\n- **1600 mg/day total**, for example 400 mg four times daily. This would fit if 1600 mg was meant as the daily total.\n\nThe order should also come from the treating prescriber, or be confirmed by them. A clinician on the medicine service can tell me the intended dose and indication. I can then enter it right away, for example ibuprofen 400 mg or 800 mg by mouth, with the frequency they specify."
      }
     ],
